< remote:      https://github.com/tacplus-dev/eventbus/pull/new/looper/eventbus-2         remote:  [2026-10-27T16:27:40Z] checkpoint of #2 (a step) pushed to looper/eventbus-2:  2 files changed, 216 insertions(+)
I stopped issue #2 without committing anything: the release workflow can't run here until someone gives GitHub Actions a credential to read the org's private repos. I've labelled the issue `needs-decision` and left it open.

**Why it's blocked:** release CI has to run `tacplus build` on the tag. In this repo that means cloning `tacplus-dev/registry`, `kernel`, `letsencrypt` and, because of `states: all`, every service's protos, and most of those repos are private. An Actions runner only has this repo's own `GITHUB_TOKEN`, which can't read them. I ran the build the way CI would (the `tacplus:2.4` image, a fresh home directory, no token). It fails on the first clone, the registry: `could not read Username for 'https://github.com'`. nsproxy and llmproxy got through their releases because neither has private dependencies. This repo has no Actions secrets, and nothing in the roadmap's decision records covers CI credentials.

**What I asked for** ([issue comment](https://github.com/tacplus-dev/eventbus/issues/2#issuecomment-6447480141)): a GitHub App owned by tacplus-dev with read-only access to repo contents, stored as org-level Actions secrets. The workflow would turn that into a short-lived token for each build. A fine-grained personal token with read access is the simpler alternative, but it expires and belongs to one person. I advised against reusing the looper's token because it has write access. Creating either needs an org owner, but I can wire a repo secret once it exists. The other private services with dependencies (kernel#7, letsencrypt#3, gateway#6, …) will hit the same wall, which is why I suggested making it an org decision.

**Ready once the credential exists:** the rest would follow llmproxy's setup. That means two release scripts (which image tags a version gets, and the release notes) with a JUnit test against sample repos, a CI workflow, and a release workflow building for amd64 and arm64 with a dry-run mode. It also needs a "Releasing" section in the README, the `v0.1.0` tag, and a dry run of `v0.1.1`.

I saved a checkpoint to `looper/eventbus-2` that says what's done and what's left, so the next run can start from there.
